# {{ project.name }}

A native macOS desktop app built with [Tauri 2](https://v2.tauri.app): a Rust core with a SQLite database and a [Vue 3](https://vuejs.org) + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS interface in the system WebView. The release build is a small `.app` and a `.dmg`.

## Requirements

- macOS 14 or newer (Apple Silicon or Intel) with the Xcode Command Line Tools (`xcode-select --install`)
- Rust 1.90 or newer (`rustup`, https://rustup.rs)
{%- if choices.docker == "full" %}
- Node.js 24 or newer with npm — on this Mac it only starts the Tauri CLI; npm packages are installed in Docker
- Docker 24 or newer with Docker Compose
{%- else %}
- Node.js 24 or newer, npm 10 or newer
{%- if choices.docker == "run" %}
- Docker 24 or newer with Docker Compose (only for the containerized check)
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## Quick start

```sh
[ -f .env ] || cp .env.example .env   # first run after cloning
{%- if choices.docker == "full" %}
docker compose build dev
docker compose run --rm dev npm install
{%- else %}
npm install
{%- endif %}
npm run dev
```

The first start compiles the Rust side (about a minute), then the app window opens. Edits to the UI (`src/`) appear instantly; edits to Rust (`src-tauri/src/`) rebuild and restart the app. Close the window or press Ctrl+C to stop.

Release build:

```sh
npm run build
open src-tauri/target/release/bundle/macos/*.app
```

The `.dmg` is in `src-tauri/target/release/bundle/dmg/`. Builds are ad-hoc signed: they run on this Mac; distributing them to others needs a Developer ID signature and notarization (see `AGENTS.md`).

## Scripts
{% if choices.docker == "full" %}
`npm run dev`, `npm run build` and `npm run tauri` run on this Mac. Run every other script in the dev container: `docker compose run --rm dev npm run <script>`.
{% endif %}
| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `npm run dev` | The app window with hot reload (Vite on port {{ ports.WEB_PORT }}) and Rust rebuilds |
| `npm run build` | The release `.app` and `.dmg` in `src-tauri/target/release/bundle/` |
| `npm run tauri -- <command>` | Any Tauri CLI command with the settings from `.env` |
| `npm test` | UI tests (Vitest) and Rust tests (`cargo test`) |
| `npm run check` | Lint (ESLint, Clippy), format check (Prettier, rustfmt), type check and all tests — the quality gate before commits |
| `npm run lint` / `npm run lint:fix` | ESLint and Clippy / ESLint auto-fix |
| `npm run format` | Format TypeScript, Vue and Rust files |
| `npm run typecheck` | `vue-tsc` for the UI and the scripts |

## Configuration

All settings live in `.env` in the project root. It is git-ignored; `.env.example` documents every key and is committed. The values are applied when the app is started with `npm run dev` or built — the installed app does not read `.env`.

| Key | Value | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `APP_NAME` | `{{ env.APP_NAME }}` | App name: window title, menu bar, Dock, `.app`/`.dmg` name and the UI |
| `APP_SLUG` | `{{ env.APP_SLUG }}` | Rust package and binary name; dev builds keep WebView data in `~/Library/WebKit/<APP_SLUG>` |
| `APP_IDENTIFIER` | `{{ env.APP_IDENTIFIER }}` | macOS bundle identifier; release builds keep data in `~/Library/Application Support/<APP_IDENTIFIER>`. Set your own reverse domain before the first release |
| `WEB_PORT` | `{{ ports.WEB_PORT }}` | Port of the Vite dev server the dev window loads |
{%- if choices.docker != "none" %}
| `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` | `{{ project.slug }}` | Docker Compose project name |
{%- endif %}
{%- if choices.docker == "full" %}
| `WEB_TOOLCHAIN` | `docker` | `npm run dev`/`build` serve and build the UI in the `dev` container |
{%- endif %}

## Project structure

```text
src/          The interface: Vue pages, components, Pinia stores, the typed client for Rust commands
src-tauri/    The app: Rust commands, services, SQLite storage, window and bundle configuration
scripts/      Development helpers (runs the Tauri CLI with the settings from .env)
test/         UI tests
data/         Database of development builds (git-ignored)
```

The example feature **Notes** shows every layer end to end: Vue page → Pinia store → API client → Tauri command → service → repository → SQL migration. The data of `npm run dev` stays in `data/`, so development never touches the data of an installed release.
